WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:05.780
So from the start of the pandemic, we've heard
the very general statement from a bunch of

00:00:05.780 --> 00:00:08.730
different people who may or may not know what
they're talking about.

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:11.799
When it warms up, the virus is going to go
away.

00:00:11.799 --> 00:00:14.339
And that suggests this kind of seasonality
to the virus.

00:00:14.339 --> 00:00:17.070
And it implies the virus can't survive in
warmer weather.

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:19.540
And we don't actually have evidence of that
at this point.

00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:22.640
And it appears to be sort of too simplistic
of a hypothesis.

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:27.470
But we do now have a very good paper that's
looking at the issue of temperature.

00:00:27.470 --> 00:00:34.380
And it has found that there appears to be
a temperature and humidity combination present

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:38.890
in the places that have become sort of centers
of infection and spread.

00:00:38.890 --> 00:00:43.660
The paper is called Temperature, Humidity
and Latitude Analysis to predict potential

00:00:43.660 --> 00:00:46.200
spread and seasonality for covered 19.

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:50.430
It's a mouthful, but essentially what they
did is they looked at the cities that had

00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:54.350
large amounts of communities spread and the
disease getting established.

00:00:54.350 --> 00:00:59.270
And they looked at the climate data from those
cities to see is there a pattern where the

00:00:59.270 --> 00:01:02.870
virus is taking hold versus where the virus
is not?

00:01:02.870 --> 00:01:05.190
And indeed, check out what they found.

00:01:05.190 --> 00:01:11.360
They found that there was significant community
spread in cities and regions along a narrow

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:17.830
East-West distribution, roughly along the
30 to 50 degree north corridor at similar

00:01:17.830 --> 00:01:23.720
weather patterns consisting of average temperatures
of 5 to 11 degrees Celsius, combined with

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:28.170
low specific and absolute humidity.

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:32.650
Going on to say there's been a lack of significant
community establishment in expected locations

00:01:32.650 --> 00:01:39.330
based only on population proximity and extensive
population interaction through travel.

00:01:39.330 --> 00:01:40.670
So it's not just the temperature.

00:01:40.670 --> 00:01:47.830
The temperature seems to be 5 to 11 see, which
is 41 to 52 Fahrenheit, but also specific

00:01:47.830 --> 00:01:49.229
humidity ranges.

00:01:49.229 --> 00:01:56.140
And in fact, places where you would expect
the virus to become established based on proximity

00:01:56.140 --> 00:01:57.369
and travel.

00:01:57.369 --> 00:02:02.759
It has not when the temperature and humidity
haven't matched up with these parameters.

00:02:02.759 --> 00:02:05.360
So this tells us a few different things.

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:11.780
This tells us there may be a seasonal component
here, but it's more complex than just temperature.

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:14.010
It's temperature and humidity.

00:02:14.010 --> 00:02:19.230
We conceivably could predict knowing what
the temperature and humidity ranges tend to

00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:21.300
be as we get into spring and fall.

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:28.580
We could predict where over the coming months
we might see more and less spread on this

00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:29.580
basis.

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:34.629
And this is one of the first more plausible
explanations, more specifically of why it

00:02:34.629 --> 00:02:42.370
got so bad in Wuhan, Italy, Iran, South Korea
and in some specific other places, Seattle

00:02:42.370 --> 00:02:43.730
as another example.

00:02:43.730 --> 00:02:49.000
Again, these are correlations we don't yet
have a causal link.

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:50.990
This is just one study.

00:02:50.990 --> 00:02:54.610
But this is also important to be considering
now.

00:02:54.610 --> 00:03:02.700
Also important is the understanding that a
lot of the United States may enter those ranges

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:05.420
as we get into spring and summer.

00:03:05.420 --> 00:03:10.180
So you have when the entire climate and temperature
thing, there's a few different elements to

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:11.180
it.

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:18.780
On one hand, there's the question of whether
the virus itself can survive as the temperature

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:19.780
goes up.

00:03:19.780 --> 00:03:23.220
So sometimes when people say, well, when it
gets warmer, the virus is going to start to

00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:25.420
die out or it's going to last less.

00:03:25.420 --> 00:03:26.420
That's a question mark.

00:03:26.420 --> 00:03:28.980
We don't know that to be the case when we
think of the flu.

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:35.450
It is true that in places like the northeast
of the U.S. where I live, the flu epidemics

00:03:35.450 --> 00:03:38.450
are tend to go away when it gets warm.

00:03:38.450 --> 00:03:44.120
But if you look at places that are warm year
round, they also still have flu transmission.

00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:47.530
The flu seems to adapt to the warmer temperatures
in those areas.

00:03:47.530 --> 00:03:53.930
So the question of whether Cauvin, 19 and
the bug that the virus that causes it can

00:03:53.930 --> 00:03:55.720
survive in warmer temperatures.

00:03:55.720 --> 00:03:57.170
That's one question.

00:03:57.170 --> 00:04:03.970
When it gets warmer, people also tend to be
outside more and more spread out on average

00:04:03.970 --> 00:04:07.900
than inside in office buildings and in close
proximity.

00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:09.590
The think that's another factor.

00:04:09.590 --> 00:04:15.489
But if we're self isolating, socially distancing
and working from home, does that make a difference?

00:04:15.489 --> 00:04:21.029
But then lastly, is this new issue of the
actual temperature and humidity ranges at

00:04:21.029 --> 00:04:24.500
which the virus is more transmissible, more
easily being transmitted?

00:04:24.500 --> 00:04:31.369
The bad news about this study, even though
it shows a possible season seasonal component,

00:04:31.369 --> 00:04:38.509
is that a lot of that belt is going to be
including much of the United States with that

00:04:38.509 --> 00:04:40.110
optimal criteria.

00:04:40.110 --> 00:04:44.680
So the odds that it simply goes away when
we get into spring and summer on the basis

00:04:44.680 --> 00:04:50.069
of temperature and humidity without additional
intervention, like social distancing, finding

00:04:50.069 --> 00:04:53.289
treatments, et cetera, is very, very low.

00:04:53.289 --> 00:04:55.449
We're going to have to keep doing.

00:04:55.449 --> 00:04:58.819
Of exhibiting behaviors that for a lot of
us aren't fun.

00:04:58.819 --> 00:05:29.860
But it's just going

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:47.569
to have

00:05:47.569 --> 00:05:51.990
to happen.

00:05:51.990 --> 00:06:14.099
There's no way around it.

